(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatological-infestations-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatological Infestations Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does the presence of crusting or scaling on a lesion indicate?","choices":["That the epidermis has been affected","That the subcutaneous tissue is involved","That the lesion is infectious in origin","That the lesion has begun to resolve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that inference is what makes crust and scale useful morphological information. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Head lice have recurred after two pediculicide courses. Which approach is described?","choices":["A third course of the same pediculicide at double the concentration","Occlusive home remedies such as mayonnaise or mineral oil alone","Oral ivermectin given as a single dose to the whole household","A multimodal approach combining physical methods with adjuvant chemical therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — increasing resistance is the stated reason. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is characteristic of fire ant venom and behaviour?","choices":["The venom is injected through hollow hairs, and contact occurs passively on brushing","The venom is cytotoxic, producing central necrosis with a surrounding blanched rim","The venom induces mast cell degranulation, and the ants usually attack in groups","The venom is a neurotoxin, and the insects sting only when their nest is disturbed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — flushing, pruritus, hives, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting and diarrhoea can follow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which age group is most affected by head lice, and how is it usually transmitted?","choices":["Adults over forty years, primarily by sexual contact and shared towels or bedding","Homeless adults, primarily by contaminated clothing that cannot be washed or changed","Infants under one year, primarily by contact with the bedding of an infested carer","Children between three and twelve years, primarily by direct head-to-head contact"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Indirect spread through combs, brushes, bedding and headgear is less common. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the first-line oral antibiotic for Lyme disease, and what is used in children and pregnancy?","choices":["Azithromycin first line, with doxycycline reserved for those who cannot tolerate it","Intravenous ceftriaxone first line for all stages, with oral agents used only for prophylaxis","Doxycycline first line, with amoxicillin as the alternative first line for early erythema migrans","Amoxicillin first line, with doxycycline reserved for children and for pregnant patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — macrolides such as azithromycin are second line, over a ten to fourteen day course. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for Rocky Mountain spotted fever?","choices":["Male sex, adults aged 40 to 64, children under 10, and rural dwelling","Female sex, adults over 65 years, urban dwelling, and winter exposure","Immunosuppression, renal failure, alcoholism and injection drug use","Obesity, diabetes and nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is prevalent in southeastern and south central states in spring and early summer. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"When is intravenous antibiotic therapy used in Lyme disease?","choices":["For some cutaneous manifestations, acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans and arthritis","For every patient at the moment the erythema migrans lesion is first recognised","For prophylaxis in anyone bitten by a tick in an endemic region of the country","For patients who cannot tolerate doxycycline, in place of a macrolide antibiotic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — ceftriaxone, cefotaxime or penicillin G are the agents named. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"By which mechanisms do caterpillars produce skin disease?","choices":["Burrowing into the stratum corneum where the organism deposits its eggs and faeces","Mechanical irritation by pointed hairs, toxin injection through hollow hairs, and hypersensitivity to hairs","Direct penetration of the skin by larvae that then migrate through the epidermal layer of the skin","Injection of a neurotoxin through a modified ovipositor when the insect is threatened"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the aggregate of these effects is called lepidopterism. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which distribution is typical of scabies in a healthy adult?","choices":["Scalp and hairline first, then spreading down the neck and onto the upper back over weeks","Ankles and wrists first, then spreading centripetally to involve the palms and the soles","Back, neck, shoulders and waist as linear excoriations, with post-inflammatory pigmentation","Interdigital webs, sides of fingers, volar wrists, elbows, axillae and genitalia, sparing head and neck"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The areolae are also involved in women. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How is a bedbug infestation diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Diagnosis is by serology; management is oral doxycycline for ten to fourteen days","Diagnosis is by physical examination; management is symptomatic with a professional exterminator required","Diagnosis is by skin scraping under mineral oil; management is topical permethrin applied overnight","Diagnosis is by dermoscopy showing ovoid eggs; management is surgical excision or cryotherapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — topical antiseptic or antibiotic for secondary infection, steroids or antihistamines for itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which organism causes Rocky Mountain spotted fever, and what are its vectors and risk factors?","choices":["Cimex lectularius, carried in luggage and second-hand mattresses; risk is higher among travellers","Rickettsia rickettsii, carried by dog ticks, wood ticks and rodents; risk is higher in males, adults 40 to 64, children under 10 and rural dwellers","Borrelia burgdorferi, carried by ticks with birds and deer as dispersal animals; risk is higher in the northeastern and upper midwestern states","Xenopsylla cheopis, carried by rats; risk is higher in crowded conditions with poor sanitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is prevalent in southeastern and south central states in spring and early summer. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which populations and regions are particularly affected by cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["The Great Lakes region, and paddy workers and rice farmers of the Far East","The American Midwest and Southeast, particularly people using attics and storage areas","The Pacific Northwest, particularly people working in basements and around wood piles","The southeastern and south central states, particularly rural dwellers in spring"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — exposure to cercaria-infested water is what all of them share. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"How soon do most patients feel relief after scabies treatment, and how long may symptoms linger?","choices":["Relief within hours, with all symptoms gone within three days","Relief only after six weeks, with itch persisting for several months","No relief until a second course has been given at four weeks","Relief in about three days, though rash and itch may last up to four weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — triamcinolone can be used for the residual itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which scabies patients are treated only where the diagnosis is documented?","choices":["Immunosuppressed patients of any age","Pregnant patients","Children under two years of age","Patients over sixty-five years of age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — otherwise all infested persons in a family or group are treated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient's scabies has not cleared after individual therapy. Which regimen is described?","choices":["A mid-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily for a month","Ivermectin every two weeks for two to three doses, with topical permethrin every three days to weekly","A third overnight application of topical permethrin given one week after the second one","Oral albendazole for three days, repeated if there is no response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same regimen used for hyperkeratotic or immunosuppressed disease. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which complications of scabies are named, and how are the persistent papules treated?","choices":["Necrotic ulceration requiring surgical reconstruction once the wound has become stable","Staphylococcal superinfection, post-scabietic papules treated with potent or intralesional steroid, and psychological effects","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, treated with supportive care while the kidney function recovers on its own","Acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans, treated with intravenous ceftriaxone over several weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — intralesional triamcinolone acetonide is given at 2.5 to 5 mg per millilitre. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which three lice cause pediculosis, and which body regions do they affect?","choices":["Sarcoptes scabiei on the scalp, Cimex lectularius on the trunk, and Phthirus pubis on pubic skin","Phthirus pubis on the scalp, Pediculus humanus capitis on pubic skin, and Cimex pilosellus on the trunk","Pediculus humanus capitis on the scalp, Pediculus humanus humanus on the trunk, Phthirus pubis on pubic skin","Pediculus humanus capitis on the trunk, Pediculus humanus humanus on the scalp, Phthirus pubis on the eyelashes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The body louse is about 30% larger than the head louse. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What do the pathological findings show in cutaneous larva migrans?","choices":["Larvae trapped within the follicular canal, stratum corneum or dermis, with an eosinophilic infiltrate","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with separation right at the dermal-epidermal junction","Granular immunoglobulin A deposits at the tips of the dermal papillae","A band-like infiltration of lymphocytes filling the papillary dermis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — light microscopy with mineral oil shows live and dead larvae in the folliculitic form. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"On what basis is cutaneous larva migrans diagnosed?","choices":["On serology for antibodies against the animal hookworm","On stool microscopy for hookworm ova and parasites","On dermoscopy showing ovoid eggs within the lesion","Clinically, on the presence of the serpentine rash"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — histology shows larvae with an eosinophilic infiltrate if biopsy is done. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How is cercarial dermatitis managed?","choices":["Symptomatically, with antihistamines, oatmeal baths, antipruritic lotions, aspirin for pain and topical or oral glucocorticoids","With albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for a course of three days, or ivermectin by mouth for one to two days","With overnight topical permethrin to the whole skin surface and a repeat dose at one week","With surgical excision or cryotherapy of each lesion together with tetanus prophylaxis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — proper washing and hygiene are also advised. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"What is the classic manifestation of stage 3, late persistent Lyme disease?","choices":["A single expanding erythematous lesion with central clearing at the site of the bite","A blanching rash spreading centripetally from the ankles and wrists to the trunk","Monoarticular or oligoarticular arthritis affecting the knee or weight-bearing joints","Cranial nerve palsies, meningitis and radiculopathies developing over days to weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — subacute encephalopathy and acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans also occur. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"How is the brown recluse spider identified and where is it abundant?","choices":["A dark fiddle or violin marking on the cephalothorax; abundant in the American Midwest and Southeast","A red hourglass on the underside of the abdomen; found in all but the most northern states","A grey herringbone pattern on the abdomen; predominant in the Pacific Northwest","A dense covering of urticating hairs; kept widely as a pet across the country"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is non-aggressive and shelters in closets, attics and stored bedding. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How is the southern black widow spider identified, and what is in its venom?","choices":["A dark violin-shaped marking on the cephalothorax; the venom is cytotoxic and locally necrotising","A grey herringbone pattern on the abdomen; the venom causes necrotic arachnidism regionally","Barbed hairs shed from the abdomen; these embed in skin and eyes causing local reactions","A red hourglass on the underside of the abdomen; the venom contains the neurotoxin alpha-latrotoxin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Latrodectus mactans is found in all but the most northern part of the country. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Describe the classic lesion of cutaneous larva migrans.","choices":["Erythematous papules in linear streaks after brushing against a caterpillar's hairs","An erythematous, raised, vesicular, linear or serpentine trail advancing 2 to 3 cm each day","A thin thread-like linear or J-shaped tunnel 1 to 10 mm long in the interdigital webs","A large expanding round lesion over 5 cm with central clearing at the site of a bite"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — intensely pruritic and painful, lasting two to eight weeks, with rare systemic symptoms. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How does a hobo spider bite present?","choices":["An immediately painful sting with erythema, swelling and urticaria developing at the site","A slowly advancing serpentine trail that progresses two to three centimetres each day","A painless bite with induration and paraesthesia within thirty minutes, a large red area, and vesicles within thirty-six hours","A painful bite with sweating and piloerection within thirty minutes, then crampy abdominal pain and muscle spasms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — eschar may follow, and systemic symptoms include headache, nausea and memory impairment. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which intravenous agents are used for the later manifestations of Lyme disease?","choices":["Vancomycin, linezolid or daptomycin","Amphotericin B or liposomal amphotericin","Intravenous immunoglobulin or cyclosporine","Ceftriaxone, cefotaxime or penicillin G"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — used for some cutaneous manifestations, acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans and arthritis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which two procedures are specifically not recommended for cutaneous larva migrans?","choices":["Surgical excision and cryotherapy","Oral albendazole and oral ivermectin","Photoprotection and emollient application","Wound dressing and simple analgesia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is also described as less effective than oral. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which reaction to bedbug bites occurs particularly in sensitised patients?","choices":["A serpentine advancing trail","Cramping abdominal pain and spasm","A bullous reaction","A necrotic ulcer with an eschar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the usual reaction is wheals and papules with a haemorrhagic punctum. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which systemic features can accompany cercarial dermatitis?","choices":["Cranial nerve palsy and meningitis","Aplastic anaemia and memory impairment","Headaches, fever and lymphangitis","Cramping abdominal pain and muscle spasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pain and swelling peak at forty-eight to seventy-two hours. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which severe local complication of tungiasis is named?","choices":["Chronic lymphoedema of the whole leg","Autoamputation of toes","Compartment syndrome of the foot","Permanent depigmentation of the sole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside pain and pruritus at the affected sites. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"}]};
